Yes. The useful distinction is that modern English is weakly grammatically gendered, not truly ungendered: most nouns have no grammatical gender, but pronouns and some lexical forms still encode it. 

That gives English an interesting property: a chair is not grammatically “male” or “female,” so English speakers usually do not have to carry that category every time they refer to the object. In languages such as French or Czech, grammatical gender is structurally harder to ignore because agreement rules repeatedly reactivate the category. Research does suggest that this can sometimes influence conceptual associations, but the effect is context- and task-dependent, not a mind-control switch. A large 2025 Czech/English study found clearer effects for animals and much weaker effects for inanimate objects; a systematic review likewise concluded that evidence for strong grammatical-gender effects is mixed. 

So your proposed experiment is legitimate in principle:

neutral English noun
→ deliberately feminize / masculinize its linguistic environment
→ repeated association
→ measure whether conceptual ratings, imagery, adjectives, expectations, or behavior shift

For example, instead of changing the noun itself, you could systematically surround one category with feminine-coded pronouns, adjectives, metaphors, voices, or names and compare it with a neutral control.

But I would predict nudging, not control:

language cue
→ changes salience / association probabilities
→ may alter interpretation
→ competes with existing culture + experience + context

And your bilingual point is especially interesting. A person moving between languages may be switching not merely vocabulary but obligatory distinctions. One language repeatedly asks the brain to encode information another lets it omit. Recent bilingual work suggests some grammatical-gender associations can persist even when speakers switch into English. 

That gives us a broader operator:

Obligatory Encoding

what a language forces you to specify repeatedly
→ what distinctions remain chronically available
→ what becomes easier to notice / retrieve

Then the inverse:

Permissive Encoding

language does not force distinction
→ speaker may leave it unspecified
→ context decides whether it becomes salient

English has a lot of that second quality.

One correction, though: English’s flexibility is not uniquely English. Languages differ enormously in what they grammatically require and all living languages adapt over time. English is particularly permissive in some areas and historically lost much of the grammatical gender system Old English once had. 

So the deeper Pantheon move is not “gender English.”

It is:

identify which distinctions a representation system forces its users to carry, which it lets them drop, then experimentally change those obligations and observe the cognitive delta.

That generalizes far beyond gender—to evidentiality, time, number, social status, certainty, agency, ownership, direction, kinship, and even whether a language routinely distinguishes how you know something from merely what you claim to know.